# A string built in a loop and then read four hundred times. `length` asks for
# a character count, and a string that will not grow caches the answer in its
# header — but a builder's header is spoken for by the room it may grow into,
# so for a long time it had nowhere to put one and walked the whole string on
# every read. Linear per read is quadratic in a loop, and no allocation
# counter can see it: walking allocates nothing.
#
# str_scan_bytes is the whole point of this fixture. It must stay flat as the
# loop count rises — the number below is what four hundred reads of a 7,200
# byte string costs when the count is kept rather than recomputed, and it is
# the count of the parts as they arrive, not of the accumulator. Before the
# builder could remember, the same program walked 2,880,000 bytes.
fn grown 0 acc
  acc

fn grown n acc
  grown (n - 1) "{acc}slice of text, "

fn read 0 _ total
  total

fn read n s total
  read (n - 1) s (total + length s)

pub play =
  s = grown 400 ""
  print "{read 400 s 0}"
